    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background: With the increasing rate of obesity and diabetes mellitus, the rates of non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) have been rapidly increasing. Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) has become the most commonly diagnosed form of liver cancer with NAFLD-related HCC cases becoming the fastest growing cause of HCC. Previous guidelines only called for HCC screening in the presence of liver cirrhosis; however, patients with NAFLD both with and without cirrhosis have been seen to develop HCC. Methodology: We conducted a retrospective cohort study using the Truven Health MarketScan® Databases to identify non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) patients to determine the incidence of HCC using Kaplan Meier methods in this population. We also used Cox regression analysis to find hazard ratios relating patient characteristics with HCC development. Results: We analyzed 746,016 patients with NAFLD (mean age 51.5±12.9 years, 46.1% male, 14.7% cirrhosis). A total of 1,116 patients developed HCC over a follow up of 2,416,834 person-years, yielding an overall HCC incidence rate per 1000 person years of 0.46 (0.44 - 0.49), 0.34 (0.31 - 0.37) for females, 0.61 (0.57 - 0.66) for males, 0.81 (0.76 - 0.87) for diabetes, 0.23 (0.20 - 0.25) for non-diabetes, 2.78 (2.61 - 2.97) for cirrhosis, and 0.08 (0.07 - 0.10) for non-cirrhosis. After adjustment for cirrhosis status, age, sex, diabetes status, and BMI, cirrhosis (adjusted hazard ratio [aHR] 24.6, 95% CI 20.9-28.9) and age ≥ 60 years (aHR 4.80, 95% CI 4.24-5.44) were the strongest factors associated with HCC development. Conclusion: Our study provided detailed NAFLD-related HCC incidence estimation and risk factor analysis. Results suggest future surveillance strategies should take into account a combination of age, sex, and diabetes alongside cirrhosis.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
